  "query_preview": "Outlook:\n\nPersistent southwest flow remains anchored in the wake of Wednesdays \nwave. A broader mid-level shortwave and upper jet streak will \ntraverse the region late Friday into Saturday. While confidence is \nlow in the exact progression of events, its worth noting the \npotential return of synoptic ascent and thus the potential for gusty \nwinds and scattered convection.\n\nThe cut off pacific low responsible for the barrage of shortwaves \nand resulting active period looks to dislodge late weekend into next \nweek. Confidence is low at the immediate impacts but there is \nincreasing chances of a regime change to broader synoptic forcing.\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWednesday Convective Winds: High confidence (>80%) in thunderstorms \nthat will pose the risk of severe gusts in excess of 60mph given \nstrong multi-ensemble agreement. Soundings area wide support \nefficient deep layer shear and strong instability for storm \nmaintenance.\n\nSW Montana High Winds: Medium Confidence (60%) in meeting High Wind \nWarning criteria in terrain-aligned valleys on Wednesday evening as \nthe peak of the 700mb jet is overhead. Confidence decreases into \nThursday as the jet streak exits north and east. \n\nFire Weather: Medium Confidence (50%) in critical conditions on \nWednesday and Thursday. Winds are the highest source of confidence, \nhumidity is our lowest with values borderline. However, deep mixing \nadvertised by the models does support values approaching critical \nvalues.\n\nExtended Regime: Low Confidence (30%) in the overall changes in the \nupper-level pattern. Cut off lows are notoriously difficult to pin \npoint in the long range as are the resulting impacts. \n\n- JR [Thnks to Eric A & Evan T for preparing me to write my 1st AFD]",
